# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# security.txt - RFC 9116
#
# A machine-readable "how to report a vulnerability to this installation". It
# is the first place a researcher or an automated scanner looks, and its
# absence is read as "there is nobody to tell", which is how a finding ends up
# on a mailing list instead of in your inbox.
#
# WHERE IT GOES
#
#   /srv/uboss/current/customer-web/.well-known/security.txt
#
# The nginx site file already serves that path - see `location =
# /.well-known/security.txt` in deploy/nginx/uboss.conf. Serve it from the
# storefront host; that is the hostname somebody who found something will be
# looking at.
#
# THREE THINGS TO CHANGE, AND ALL THREE MATTER
#
#   1. Contact:  your own monitored address. A group, not a person - somebody
#      is always on holiday. A bounce means the finding gets published instead.
#   2. Expires:  a real date, LESS THAN A YEAR from now. An expired file is
#      treated as no policy at all by every tool that reads it, so put the
#      renewal in the same calendar as the TLS certificate.
#   3. Policy:   where your written policy lives. SECURITY.md in this
#      repository is the starting text; publish it somewhere a stranger can
#      read without a login.
#
# OPTIONAL BUT WORTH IT
#
#   Encryption:  a URL to your PGP public key, so a report containing a
#                working exploit does not travel as plain email.
#   Acknowledgments: a page naming the people who have reported things. It
#                costs nothing and it is most of why people report to you
#                rather than about you.
#
# SIGN IT, IF YOU CAN. RFC 9116 allows a detached PGP signature over this
# file, which stops somebody who can write to your web root from redirecting
# reports to themselves. `gpg --clearsign security.txt` and serve the result.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

Contact: mailto:security@your-company.example
Expires: 2027-01-01T00:00:00.000Z
Preferred-Languages: en
Canonical: https://shop.your-company.example/.well-known/security.txt
Policy: https://your-company.example/security-policy

# Encryption: https://your-company.example/pgp-key.txt
# Acknowledgments: https://your-company.example/security-thanks
